hi in this video I'm going to  demonstrate the optical sectioning  property of our confocal microscope or  any good kind of aperture microscope so  in the left-hand side you can see there  is an animal cell and in the right hand  side in the inset you can see the cross  sectional view so optical sectioning is  a property of confocal microscope which  actually doesn't section the specimen  but it's sectioning the specimen  optically that means it's imaging  separate focal plane at several time  points so if you look at the image on  the left side you can see due to the  motorized stage of the microscope the  flop focal plane is changing every time  point and on the insect you can see that  cellular details is also changing now  the cellular details is significant  amount when it goes into the deeper  focal plane the amount of the cellular  details is decreasing and the microscope  would take the image from the deeper  focal plane to the topmost focal plane  and reconstruct the whole image into a  maximum intensity projection and we  would see a maximum intensity projection  map of that particular cell and this is  how the confocal microscope optical  sectioning works I hope this video was  useful for you if you like my video  let's give it a quick thumbs up and  don't forget to subscribe thank you
 

